---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/layout/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
import AnalyticsDashboard from '../components/analytics/AnalyticsDashboard.tsx';
import { ANALYTICS_ALTERNATES } from '../i18n/alternates';
---

<Layout
  title="Trading Analytics Dashboard | Stockade"
  description="Analyze your paper trading performance with equity curves, win rates, drawdown metrics & trade journals on Stockade's free simulator."
  alternates={ANALYTICS_ALTERNATES}
>
  <Navbar alternates={ANALYTICS_ALTERNATES} />
  <main class="max-w-[1400px] mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="text-[11px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[#f59e0b] mb-2">Analytics</div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--c-text)]">Performance Dashboard</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-[var(--c-text-subtle)] text-[14px]">Your simulated trading statistics — equity curve, win rate, drawdown, and trade journal.</p>
    </div>
    <AnalyticsDashboard client:load />

    <section class="mt-16 pt-12 border-t border-[var(--c-border)] max-w-[820px]">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--c-text)] mb-4">
        Understanding Your Trading Metrics
      </h2>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Every number on this dashboard is calculated from the trades you have closed in Stockade's
        paper trading simulator and stored locally in your browser. Knowing what each figure actually measures is
        the difference between reviewing your trading and admiring the P&amp;L — <a href="/blog/analyze-trading-performance-metrics/" class="text-[#f59e0b] hover:underline">analyzing your trading performance</a> walks through win rate, profit factor, and drawdown in more depth.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-4">Headline statistics</h3>
      <dl class="space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Total P&amp;L</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The sum of realised profit and loss across every closed trade. Open positions are not
            counted until you close them, so a flattering total can hide a losing position still on
            the books.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Equity</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            Your current account value — the $100,000 starting balance plus total P&amp;L.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Win rate</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The share of closed trades that finished in profit. A high win rate is reassuring but means
            little on its own: a strategy that wins 80% of the time still loses money if the 20% of
            losers are large enough.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Profit factor</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            Gross profit divided by gross loss. Anything above 1.0× means you took out more than you
            gave back, and 1.5× or better is generally considered solid. It answers the question win
            rate cannot — how much you earn per unit of loss absorbed.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Average win</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The mean profit on your winning trades. Read it against your average loss: if your average
            win is the smaller of the two, you need a correspondingly high win rate simply to break
            even.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="text-[14px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)]">Maximum drawdown</dt>
          <dd class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
            The largest peak-to-trough fall in your equity, expressed as a percentage. This is the pain
            metric. It estimates the worst stretch you would have had to sit through — in real trading,
            usually what ends a strategy long before the maths does. <a href="/blog/risk-management-position-sizing/" class="text-[#f59e0b] hover:underline">Position sizing and the 1% rule</a> covers the recovery math behind why drawdown matters so much.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">Equity curve</h3>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The equity curve plots your account value after each closed trade. Its shape matters more than
        its endpoint: a steady climb points to a process you can repeat, while a flat line broken by
        one or two enormous jumps usually points to luck.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">Trade journal</h3>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The journal lists every closed trade with its symbol, direction, entry and exit price, size,
        P&amp;L and holding time. The average duration figure is a useful reality check: many traders
        discover they are holding losers far longer than winners, or trading a much shorter timeframe
        than they intended.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-[15px] font-semibold text-[var(--c-text)] mt-8 mb-3">Time heatmap</h3>
      <p class="text-[15px] text-[var(--c-text-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The heatmap breaks your results down by day of the week and hour of the day, colouring each
        cell by net P&amp;L. Given enough trades it can show that one particular session is quietly
        responsible for most of your losses. Treat a thin sample with suspicion — a single cell needs
        dozens of trades behind it before the colour means anything.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
